    intro: `Most homes in La Quinta's private golf communities are not primary residences. They are winter homes for owners whose main addresses are in coastal California, the Bay Area, the Pacific Northwest, Canada or the northern states — people who arrive in November and leave by May. Buying as a seasonal owner is a different exercise from buying a primary home, and the mistakes tend to be different too: not the house, but the paperwork, the rules and the seven months you are not there. The La Quinta Snowbird Buyer's Checklist is built around those gaps. Residency and tax. California taxes residents on worldwide income, and owning a home here does not by itself make you a resident — but the line between "second home" and "residency" is drawn on facts and days, not intent. The checklist explains what the California Franchise Tax Board looks at, why record-keeping matters from the first season, and why "I moved to La Quinta for the taxes" is a sentence that should never be said (our blog post La Quinta Isn't a Tax Move covers this in depth). It also introduces Proposition 13 — California's property-tax framework, under which your assessed value is set at purchase and then limited in annual growth — and what that means for a long-hold second home. Out-of-state and Canadian buyers get a specific section on cross-border ownership questions to raise with their advisers. HOA and club pitfalls for absentee owners. Every La Quinta community has rules on what an absent owner must maintain, how landscaping is handled, who may access the property, and whether and how it can be rented. The checklist covers: reading the CC&Rs for absentee-owner obligations; understanding what the HOA does and does not maintain for attached versus detached housing; the City of La Quinta's short-term-rental regulations and how they interact with HOA rules; and club membership tiers designed for seasonal members, where they exist. Summer readiness. La Quinta summers regularly exceed 110°F, and a home left unattended through them needs a plan: HVAC set points and monitoring, water shut-off and leak detection, pool and landscape service, pest control, vehicle storage, mail and package handling, and a local property manager or trusted contact with access through the gate. The checklist gives a room-by-room close-down sequence and an opening sequence for the return. Practical readiness. Utilities and how to set them up from out of state; homeowners and earthquake insurance considerations; vehicle registration and driver's license questions; establishing local medical care; and setting up the household so it can be operated remotely. The checklist is delivered as a PDF by email. Requesting it also introduces you to our partner California-licensed real-estate professional in La Quinta, who works with seasonal buyers routinely.`,
