    intro: `Few cities anywhere have a roster of golf architects like La Quinta's. Inside one city limit you can play Pete Dye's tournament stadium, Tom Fazio in two entirely different idioms, Jack Nicklaus, Greg Norman, Tom Weiskopf, Arnold Palmer's only desert design, a Rees Jones original, a Clive Clark, and a 1959 Lawrence Hughes routing that predates all of them. For a buyer choosing a community, the architect is not trivia. It is the single best predictor of how the course will feel on a Tuesday morning for the next twenty years. Why the architect matters to a home buyer. A course's designer determines its difficulty from the member tees, its walkability, its visual character, how it plays for the mid-handicap golfer who makes up most of a club's membership, and how it ages. A Dye course and a Palmer course ask fundamentally different things of the same player, and a member who loves one may find the other exhausting or dull. Because most La Quinta clubs have a single course, the architect is the golf you are buying. The roster, and where each left a mark. Pete Dye — the Stadium Course at PGA West (1986), host of The American Express and famously punitive from the back tees; the Dunes at PGA West; and one of the two courses at The Hideaway, with his signature visual intimidation and hard-cut bunkering. Tom Fazio — The Madison Club, a restrained, wide-fairway, view-framed routing; and The Quarry at La Quinta, a vertical, rock-close design built through a former quarry — the same architect in two opposite moods. Jack Nicklaus — the Nicklaus Tournament course and a private Nicklaus course at PGA West, rewarding second-shot positioning over heroic recovery. Greg Norman — the Norman Course at PGA West, links-influenced with fescue edges. Tom Weiskopf — the Weiskopf Private at PGA West, big-shouldered championship traditional. Arnold Palmer — The Tradition Golf Club (1997), his only Coachella Valley design, conceived to be walkable with open green fronts. Rees Jones — Andalusia Country Club, classically proportioned in the idiom of his U.S. Open restorations. Clive Clark — the second course at The Hideaway, the friendlier daily-play companion to Dye's. Lawrence Hughes — La Quinta Country Club (1959), a classic mid-century traditional that has matured into the most settled member experience in the city. What the full guide covers. A profile of each architect and his design philosophy; a hole-by-hole sense of the signature features on each La Quinta course; how each course plays from the member tees rather than the tips; which are walkable; how the courses have been renovated over time; and a matching guide — which architect suits the low-handicap competitor, the social golfer, the walker, the player who wants variety, and the player who wants the same beautiful eighteen every day. The guide is delivered as a PDF by email. Requesting it also introduces you to our partner California-licensed real-estate professional in La Quinta. For the club-by-club view, pair it with the La Quinta Private Club Comparison Guide.`,
